    <h1 style="font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Julian Perez — Press</h1>
    <p style="color:#555;margin-bottom:3rem;">Media coverage, reviews and interviews.</p>

    <!-- FEATURED ARTICLES -->

    <article style="margin-bottom:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;">
      <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">Resident Advisor · October 20, 2016</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
        <a href="https://ra.co/news/36928" style="color:#111;">Julian Perez Releases Debut LP, Closes Fathers &amp; Sons Productions</a>
      </h2>
      <p>Julian Perez released his debut album <em>Solemnity</em>, a vinyl-only double LP and the final release on his imprint Fathers &amp; Sons Productions. The label, established in 2012, closed after twelve releases featuring artists including S.A.M., Livio &amp; Roby, and Brian Harden. The 11-track album spans four sides of vinyl, demonstrating Perez's attention to detail and the evolution of his groove-led style — from atmospheric soundscapes and electro-influenced 909 drum patterns to deep dub techno and club-oriented compositions. The album's title references "the state of being serious and dignified."</p>
      <p><strong>Tracklist:</strong> A1: Ain't That The Truth? / A2: Xless / A3: The Unspoken Thing / B1: Unusual Sense / B2: That Stuck TDK / B3: Interfluide / C1: Alog / C2: A War Melody Dreamer / D1: Sending Forth / D2: Recognition! / D3: Peace In The Darkness</p>
    </article>

    <article style="margin-bottom:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;">
      <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">XLR8R · December 16, 2016</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
        <a href="https://xlr8r.com/news/stream-snippets-of-julian-perezs-debut-album/" style="color:#111;">Stream Snippets of Julian Perez's Debut Album — Solemnity</a>
      </h2>
      <p>XLR8R covered the release of <em>Solemnity</em>, Julian Perez's debut album on Fathers &amp; Sons Productions, calling it a showcase of "Perez's attention to detail and the evolution of his groove-led style." The album spans two 12-inch vinyl records containing 11 tracks. The label had been active for four years, previously featuring artists like Livio &amp; Roby, S.A.M., and Brian Harden.</p>
    </article>

    <article style="margin-bottom:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;">
      <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">Trommel Music · May 17, 2021</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
        <a href="https://trommelmusic.com/music/reviews/review-julian-perez-break-even-lp-girada010/" style="color:#111;">Review: Julian Perez – Break-Even LP [GIRADA010]</a>
      </h2>
      <p>Trommel Music reviewed <em>Break-Even</em>, the landmark tenth release on Julian Perez's Girada Unlimited imprint. A double 12" vinyl showcasing his versatility across multiple electronic music genres — from spacey breakbeats and lush atmospherics to deep house grooves and dream house. The review highlighted "Energy Fever" as "a sublime piece of dream house more than capable of entrancing a dancefloor" and "CSO All The Way" as "a shimmering piece of cosmic breaks." The album spans four sides with tracks including Sweentro, Facts, Revelation, Eveileb, and Drowned In Faith (Love &amp; Light).</p>
    </article>

    <article style="margin-bottom:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;">
      <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">Resident Advisor · March 18, 2016</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
        <a href="https://ra.co/reviews/18750" style="color:#111;">Julian Perez – Now, After [Review]</a>
      </h2>
      <p>Resident Advisor reviewed Julian Perez's EP <em>Now, After</em> on Raum...musik (MUSIK096), describing it as versatile material for DJs managing the final stages of evening events. Reviewer Matt Unicomb noted Perez's "straightforward" approach — creating music suited for extended club sets. The title track features "bright keys and dubby synths" for an optimistic sonic palette, while B-side "Clue Me In" focuses on massive bass lines. The review noted that Perez had established himself through his work with Ibiza's Fathers &amp; Sons Productions label, producing sought-after vinyl that commands premium prices on the secondary market.</p>
    </article>

    <article style="margin-bottom:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;">
      <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">Resident Advisor · September 12, 2014</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
        <a href="https://ra.co/news/26382" style="color:#111;">RA Ibiza Weekly: Julian Perez, The Zoo Project</a>
      </h2>
      <p>Resident Advisor's Carlos Hawthorn profiled Julian Perez as one of 2014's breakthrough talents in Ibiza, having moved to the island in 2006 and establishing himself within the underground scene as a resident DJ and co-founder of the Fathers &amp; Sons label.</p>
      <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid #ddd;padding-left:1rem;margin:1rem 0;color:#444;">
        "The way I see everything is changing, I don't know how to explain it. I feel like I'm on another level when I make music, when I DJ, it feels different. More confident." — Julian Perez
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid #ddd;padding-left:1rem;margin:1rem 0;color:#444;">
        "I think it's important for me as a DJ to always be playing different slots, and to be playing different shades of my sound, be it deeper or more big-room." — Julian Perez
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid #ddd;padding-left:1rem;margin:1rem 0;color:#444;">
        "You have to take care of yourself. I feel like self-discipline is a very important part of my job." — Julian Perez
      </blockquote>
    </article>

    <article style="margin-bottom:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;">
      <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">Trommel Music · July 12, 2018</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
        <a href="https://trommelmusic.com/featured/a-day-in-the-life-julian-perez" style="color:#111;">A Day In The Life: Julian Perez</a>
      </h2>
      <p>Alex Landzee followed Julian Perez during a weekend visit to Rotterdam, where he was invited to perform a 4-hour extended set at BAR Rotterdam (featuring a Void sound system). The feature described the Valencian-born producer as someone who carefully selects his professional engagements while maintaining active involvement in both DJing and studio work, receiving international bookings across Europe and North America. The article noted his two labels — Fathers and Sons and the newer Girada Unlimited, exploring experimental approaches within deep house and techno.</p>
    </article>

    <article style="margin-bottom:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;">
      <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">XLR8R · November 6, 2018</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
        <a href="https://xlr8r.com/news/premiere-hear-girada-unlimiteds-cure-music-debut/" style="color:#111;">Premiere: Hear Girada Unlimited's Cure Music Debut</a>
      </h2>
      <p>XLR8R premiered Girada Unlimited's seventh release on Cure Music. The A-side track "Zapatac" — a rework of Velodrome's 1988 composition "Capitaz" — gained significant traction among prominent DJs including Ricardo Villalobos, who included it in his BBC Radio 1 Essential Mix. The label drew inspiration from 1980s-90s Valencia club recordings and the nostalgia for classic Spanish nightclub culture from "La ruta del bakalao."</p>
    </article>

